# Commits whose diff is formatting only, so `git blame` should look past them.
#
# Git does not read this file on its own.  Point it here once per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# or pass --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs to a single blame.  GitHub
# reads the file automatically.
#
# The rule for adding an entry: the commit changes no behaviour, and that can
# be checked.  For a reformat, compare the AST of every touched file before and
# after with string literals masked.  A commit that merely *looks* mechanical
# does not qualify -- skipping it would hide a real change from every future
# investigation.
#
# Measured on this entry, over src/**/*.py at the commit that added this file:
# 1740 of 91573 lines (1.90%) blamed to it without this file, 1678 (1.83%)
# with it.  The file recovers the lines whose text is unchanged across the
# reformat; it cannot recover the majority, which are blank lines and closing
# brackets that blame pins to the commit that reindented them, and which
# `git blame -w` handles about as well.  It is kept because it is the standing
# instruction for future reformats -- and because GitHub and most editors honour
# it without being asked.
#
# ruff format over the whole tree.  20563 insertions across 128 files, none of
# them changing a syntax tree: the only AST difference is docstring
# continuation-line indentation, which ruff reindents along with the code.
